meta {
  name: AutoReserveringShortfall
  type: http
  seq: 62
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/web/bon/{{autoresBonnr}}/lijn
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-Auth-Token: {{authToken}}
  Content-Type: application/json
  Accept: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "artnr": "100%.126397",
    "aantal": 100,
    "teLeveren": 100,
    "vprijs": 5.0
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.bonnr: eq {{autoresBonnr}}
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setVar("autoresShortfallOk", res.body.gereserv < res.body.teLeveren);
}

docs {
  Iteratie C - automatische voorraadreservering, stock-shortfall path: a
  plain (non-BOM) artikel with only 2 in stock (artikel.aantal = 2,
  besteld-kl = 0, gereserveerd = 0 in the shared LunaNew test DB at the
  time this test was written) against a requested teLeveren of 100. This
  is deliberately NOT an error (201, no 4xx) - the design is "reserve
  what you can" - bonlijn.gereserv ends up at 2 (< teLeveren). Bruno's
  free-tier assert syntax can't do a field-to-field comparison, so the
  post-response script stores the comparison result into
  {{autoresShortfallOk}} for manual/CI inspection instead of a hard
  assert; expected gereserv for this specific artnr at implementation
  time was exactly 2. Requires {{autoresBonnr}} (run
  "AutoReserveringSetupBon" first). NOTE: if artikel "100%.126397"'s
  stock in the shared test DB has since changed (e.g. another test wrote
  to it), gereserv will differ - the important invariant to check is
  gereserv < teLeveren and res.status = 201, not the exact number 2.
}
